INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#2', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 2, 'Source snapshot',
  '## Source snapshot

- Project: `memovai/mimiclaw`
- Public site: `mimiclaw.io`
- Positioning: OpenClaw-inspired AI assistant on ESP32-S3 microcontroller hardware.
- Repository created: 2026-02-04 via GitHub API.
- GitHub API observed 2026-05-17:
  - stars: 5,412
  - forks: 791
  - open issues: 96
  - license: MIT
  - primary language: C
  - latest push: 2026-04-21T17:37:01Z
- Latest release observed: `v0.1.1`, published 2026-03-17.
- YouTube video: `MimiClaw: Run this OpenClaw Variant on a $5 Chip`, Fahd Mirza, published 2026-02-22, duration 9:13.', 'c4edc0e9379fcc062f92ae872109cde06675cca6ee799fe52b1f3d32e2def2a8', 104
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Source snapshot

- Project: `memovai/mimiclaw`
- Public site: `mimiclaw.io`
- Positioning: OpenClaw-inspired AI assistant on ESP32-S3 microcontroller hardware.
- Repository created: 2026-02-04 via GitHub API.
- GitHub API observed 2026-05-17:
  - stars: 5,412
  - forks: 791
  - open issues: 96
  - license: MIT
  - primary language: C
  - latest push: 2026-04-21T17:37:01Z
- Latest release observed: `v0.1.1`, published 2026-03-17.
- YouTube video: `MimiClaw: Run this OpenClaw Variant on a $5 Chip`, Fahd Mirza, published 2026-02-22, duration 9:13.', 'Source snapshot', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#2');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#3', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 3, 'Primary-source findings',
  '## Primary-source findings

- MimiClaw targets ESP32-S3 boards with 16 MB flash and 8 MB PSRAM.
- The runtime removes the normal server/OS layer: no Linux and no Node.js; the implementation is C on ESP-IDF/FreeRTOS.
- User interaction is mainly through Telegram; LAN access is also exposed through a WebSocket gateway on port 18789.
- The architecture uses separate queues/tasks for Telegram polling, agent loop, outbound dispatch and serial CLI.
- The agent loop builds context from files such as `SOUL.md`, `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md`, daily notes and tool guidance.
- Session history is stored as JSONL-like files in SPIFFS.
- It implements a ReAct-style tool loop with tool calling and web search through Tavily or Brave.
- It supports Anthropic and OpenAI/GPT-style providers, but the LLM itself runs in the cloud out of the box.
- Flash storage is used for firmware, OTA partitions, SPIFFS memory/session/config files and coredumps.
- The README describes runtime configuration via serial CLI stored in NVS, while `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` still says configuration is build-time only. Treat the README/release/TODO as fresher than that architecture detail.', 'f7e4c87ef6d082bc5bf1c2592510799c7fde058f474afd124d3170549aebfb64', 239
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Primary-source findings

- MimiClaw targets ESP32-S3 boards with 16 MB flash and 8 MB PSRAM.
- The runtime removes the normal server/OS layer: no Linux and no Node.js; the implementation is C on ESP-IDF/FreeRTOS.
- User interaction is mainly through Telegram; LAN access is also exposed through a WebSocket gateway on port 18789.
- The architecture uses separate queues/tasks for Telegram polling, agent loop, outbound dispatch and serial CLI.
- The agent loop builds context from files such as `SOUL.md`, `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md`, daily notes and tool guidance.
- Session history is stored as JSONL-like files in SPIFFS.
- It implements a ReAct-style tool loop with tool calling and web search through Tavily or Brave.
- It supports Anthropic and OpenAI/GPT-style providers, but the LLM itself runs in the cloud out of the box.
- Flash storage is used for firmware, OTA partitions, SPIFFS memory/session/config files and coredumps.
- The README describes runtime configuration via serial CLI stored in NVS, while `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` still says configuration is build-time only. Treat the README/release/TODO as fresher than that architecture detail.', 'Primary-source findings', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#3');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#4', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 4, 'Video findings',
  '## Video findings

- The video frames MimiClaw as the most extreme OpenClaw-family shrink: from Mac mini/server agents down to microcontroller hardware.
- It emphasizes the two-core split: networking and Telegram on one side, agent processing on the other.
- It correctly stresses the board requirement: ESP32-S3, not generic ESP32, with 16 MB flash and 8 MB PSRAM.
- It flags a practical hardware issue: ESP32-S3 boards may expose different USB/JTAG/UART ports, and flashing through the wrong port can fail.
- It highlights the honest caveat: the board is cheap and low-power, but API calls still go to cloud LLM providers.', 'c2a3485523e817149d728049c2f51aac6ac097d466ead708e04525b05d549a01', 137
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Video findings

- The video frames MimiClaw as the most extreme OpenClaw-family shrink: from Mac mini/server agents down to microcontroller hardware.
- It emphasizes the two-core split: networking and Telegram on one side, agent processing on the other.
- It correctly stresses the board requirement: ESP32-S3, not generic ESP32, with 16 MB flash and 8 MB PSRAM.
- It flags a practical hardware issue: ESP32-S3 boards may expose different USB/JTAG/UART ports, and flashing through the wrong port can fail.
- It highlights the honest caveat: the board is cheap and low-power, but API calls still go to cloud LLM providers.', 'Video findings', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#4');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#5', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 5, 'Feature gaps and caveats from TODO',
  '## Feature gaps and caveats from TODO

- The project TODO says Telegram user allowlist is not implemented yet; this is security-critical because anyone who can message the bot may consume API credits or trigger agent behavior.
- Agent-driven memory persistence appears partially incomplete or evolving; memory files exist, but implementation status differs across docs.
- More file tools, subagents/background tasks, media handling, richer skills, bootstrap alignment and full cron/heartbeat behavior are still listed as gaps or staged work.
- The project is moving quickly, and documentation is not fully synchronized.', '6ee62d70626ad2ff6a9ace836a405fe2d8e35bbb8e4fd5b060cbecceb3fd8c49', 123
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Feature gaps and caveats from TODO

- The project TODO says Telegram user allowlist is not implemented yet; this is security-critical because anyone who can message the bot may consume API credits or trigger agent behavior.
- Agent-driven memory persistence appears partially incomplete or evolving; memory files exist, but implementation status differs across docs.
- More file tools, subagents/background tasks, media handling, richer skills, bootstrap alignment and full cron/heartbeat behavior are still listed as gaps or staged work.
- The project is moving quickly, and documentation is not fully synchronized.', 'Feature gaps and caveats from TODO', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#5');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#6', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 6, 'What is useful for Techscope',
  '## What is useful for Techscope

- Embedded agents are a separate environment class, not just a smaller server runtime.
- Severe constraints force a clean separation of:
  - channel adapters;
  - message queues;
  - agent loop;
  - memory/session storage;
  - tool registry;
  - configuration surface;
  - OTA/update path.
- Plain files for identity/memory/tasks are still useful even on tiny hardware.
- Always-on agent design can be extremely cheap and low power, but autonomy shifts risk toward physical-device security, token leakage, firmware update safety and channel authorization.', 'd1f283947c8813a8ae3d3107e32d755fe641bf7400bffa311eac9622380ae9c0', 118
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## What is useful for Techscope

- Embedded agents are a separate environment class, not just a smaller server runtime.
- Severe constraints force a clean separation of:
  - channel adapters;
  - message queues;
  - agent loop;
  - memory/session storage;
  - tool registry;
  - configuration surface;
  - OTA/update path.
- Plain files for identity/memory/tasks are still useful even on tiny hardware.
- Always-on agent design can be extremely cheap and low power, but autonomy shifts risk toward physical-device security, token leakage, firmware update safety and channel authorization.', 'What is useful for Techscope', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#6');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note#7', '2026-05-17-mimiclaw-source-note', 7, 'Source-quality notes',
  '## Source-quality notes

- GitHub/README/release/TODO are primary sources.
- The website is project-owned but marketing-heavy.
- The video is useful secondary explanation, but not authoritative for implementation state.
