Status: draft
Recommendation: standard', 'Assessment: Skills vs MCP for agent tooling', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#1');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#2', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', 2, 'One-paragraph read',
  '## One-paragraph read

This is a high-value source for Techscope because it gives a crisp operational distinction: Skills are best for reusable process knowledge and local executable workflows, while MCP is best for standardized external-service integration and centralized tool boundaries. The video''s title is intentionally provocative, but the actual conclusion is balanced and useful: Skills do not kill MCP; they reduce unnecessary MCP usage and pair well with MCP where the server provides access and the skill provides procedure.', '2aaca095eaf6e02d095be2e2c59c5ea9bacdc288a64ce59d34c705949f1d5771', 107
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## One-paragraph read

This is a high-value source for Techscope because it gives a crisp operational distinction: Skills are best for reusable process knowledge and local executable workflows, while MCP is best for standardized external-service integration and centralized tool boundaries. The video''s title is intentionally provocative, but the actual conclusion is balanced and useful: Skills do not kill MCP; they reduce unnecessary MCP usage and pair well with MCP where the server provides access and the skill provides procedure.', 'One-paragraph read', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#2');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#3', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', 3, 'Why it matters',
  '## Why it matters

- We are actively designing agents that need Telegram ingestion, source processing, web lookup, local scripts, Obsidian/Markdown memory and future coding workflows.
- Bad default choice here creates long-term drag: too many MCP servers can bloat context and operations; too many ad hoc skills/scripts can create security and consistency problems.
- This material can become a reusable decision matrix for every future agent project.', '5182a0f4b142052e367602d8e4de4147b9ec547a525990e46c78ba3257c3c7c4', 92
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Why it matters

- We are actively designing agents that need Telegram ingestion, source processing, web lookup, local scripts, Obsidian/Markdown memory and future coding workflows.
- Bad default choice here creates long-term drag: too many MCP servers can bloat context and operations; too many ad hoc skills/scripts can create security and consistency problems.
- This material can become a reusable decision matrix for every future agent project.', 'Why it matters', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#3');

INSERT INTO chunks (id, document_id, ordinal, heading, text, hash, token_count)
VALUES (
  '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#4', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', 4, 'Technical claims',
  '## Technical claims

- Skills use progressive disclosure and can keep context footprint smaller than always-loaded tool definitions.
- Skills should encode repeatable procedures, local command workflows, project conventions and script usage.
- MCP should be used when a durable protocol/server boundary is needed.
- OAuth/refresh tokens, shared team centralization, caching/rate-limit management and audit/governance are strong MCP signals.
- CLI-backed tools such as `gh` can often be wrapped by skills instead of duplicated as MCP servers.
- Skills and MCP are complementary: use MCP for access, skills for process.', '2d21f78404eeac4c450ddfa94740e2231959248e3f48052c8d3c9e7271439dfb', 121
);
INSERT INTO chunks_fts (text, heading, document_id, chunk_id)
VALUES ('## Technical claims

- Skills use progressive disclosure and can keep context footprint smaller than always-loaded tool definitions.
- Skills should encode repeatable procedures, local command workflows, project conventions and script usage.
- MCP should be used when a durable protocol/server boundary is needed.
- OAuth/refresh tokens, shared team centralization, caching/rate-limit management and audit/governance are strong MCP signals.
- CLI-backed tools such as `gh` can often be wrapped by skills instead of duplicated as MCP servers.
- Skills and MCP are complementary: use MCP for access, skills for process.', 'Technical claims', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment', '2026-05-17-skills-vs-mcp-agent-tooling-assessment#4');

